// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-09-17 (Thursday): Pure math sequence — Pascal's triangle
// ============================================================================
//
// THE SEQUENCE: four consecutive rows of Pascal's triangle, n=3..6:
//   row3: 1 3 3 1        (sum = 8  = 2^3)
//   row4: 1 4 6 4 1       (sum = 16 = 2^4)
//   row5: 1 5 10 10 5 1    (sum = 32 = 2^5)
//   row6: 1 6 15 20 15 6 1  (sum = 64 = 2^6)
// Two real properties of the triangle drive the piece:
//   1. Every row is a PALINDROME — read forwards or backwards, same numbers.
//      That symmetry becomes the melodic shape directly: each phrase below IS
//      its own retrograde, not a device applied after the fact.
//   2. Each row SUMS to 2^n — row3 (index 3, sum 8) gives the kick's own
//      euclid(3,8), so the one percussion pulse in this piece is read
//      straight off the math, not chosen and then justified.
// Rows 5 and 6 have coefficients too large to use directly as scale degrees,
// so they're taken mod 8: row5 -> 1 5 2 2 5 1, row6 -> 1 6 7 4 7 6 1. Where a
// value repeats back-to-back (row5's "2 2", row3's "3 3"), the repeat is
// raised an octave instead of restated flat — same grace-note move
// 2026-09-10's twin-primes file used, here motivated by wanting the repeated
// coefficient to still read as a real leap rather than a held note.
//
// PHRASE DEVELOPMENT: the four rows alternate one per cycle (<...>), so the
// piece literally builds the triangle outward — 4 notes, then 5, then 6, then
// 7 — and the rest-density falls as the rows widen (3 rests -> 2 -> 2 -> 1),
// mirroring the triangle actually filling in. Real leaps throughout (up to a
// 6th, plus the octave leaps from the embellishments) from each row's own
// symmetric rise/fall — no scale-exercise stepwise runs.
//
// SCALE: D:dorian = Kafi thaat (S R g M P D n — komal Ga, komal Ni).
//
// ARRANGEMENT — deliberately different from the last Thursday slot
// (2026-09-10, twin primes): that file built a full dub-techno kit (kick +
// hard-ducked sub + hi-hats + supersaw lead + separate pad, three-way
// duckorbit chain) in E phrygian. Today's math only earns ONE percussion
// voice (the euclid(3,8) kick straight off row3), so the kit stays that
// small rather than padding it out with a sub/hats by habit — per
// MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md, a derived number needing an audible outlet doesn't
// justify a full drum kit as that outlet. Square wave lead (sharp/abrasive,
// MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §1) against a triangle pad (pure/soft) for real
// blend/contrast, swapping out last week's supersaw-vs-sawtooth pairing.
// One automation layer: the lead's filter opens across the 8-cycle pattern
// in step with the rows widening, so the triangle's own growth is audible in
// the timbre, not just the note count. Light duckorbit from the kick onto
// lead + pad — real sidechain (MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §10), not decorative.
//
// CYCLES: 8 (two passes through the 4-row alternation). setcpm(30) x 8 / 60
// = 16s.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(30)

$lead: n(`<
  [1 ~ ~ 3 ~ ~ 10 1]
  [1 ~ 4 ~ 6 4 ~ 1]
  [1 5 ~ 2 9 5 ~ 1]
  [1 6 7 ~ 4 7 6 1]
>`)
  .scale("D:dorian")
  .sound("square")
  .attack(.01).decay(.12).sustain(.2).release(.25)
  .lpf(sine.range(1000, 2400).slow(8))
  .lpq(6)
  .gain(.34)
  .room(1)
  .orbit(2)

$pad: n("[0,4]").scale("D:dorian")
  .sound("triangle")
  .attack(.4).release(2)
  .lpf(900)
  .gain(.16)
  .room(3)
  .orbit(3)

$: s("bd").euclid(3, 8)
  .duckorbit("2:3").duckattack("0.02:0.08").duckdepth("0.5:0.35")
  .gain(.4)
  .room(.3)
